1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,976
Three kings in one year.

2
00:00:01,976 --> 00:00:06,897
In 1066, England was ruled by three
different kings, one after another.

3
00:00:08,198 --> 00:00:11,622
One of them marched his army
about 300 kilometres north,

4
00:00:11,622 --> 00:00:15,352
and won one of the most famous
victories in English history.

5
00:00:15,352 --> 00:00:19,184
Nineteen days later, he had to fight
again, on a hill near Hastings.

6
00:00:20,484 --> 00:00:20,766
Beef.

7
00:00:20,766 --> 00:00:21,050
Army.

8
00:00:21,050 --> 00:00:21,504
Justice.

9
00:00:21,504 --> 00:00:22,525
Even the word war.

10
00:00:22,525 --> 00:00:27,349
What happened on that hill is a big part of
the reason these words are English today.

11
00:00:27,349 --> 00:00:31,832
And it pushed English out of the halls
of power for nearly three hundred years.

12
00:00:33,131 --> 00:00:34,448
How War Made English.

13
00:00:34,448 --> 00:00:35,139
Part three.

14
00:00:35,139 --> 00:00:36,018
1066.

15
00:00:36,018 --> 00:00:39,594
Three kings, two great battles,
and a flood of new words.

16
00:00:40,893 --> 00:00:43,625
At the end of part two,
we found a family secret.

17
00:00:43,625 --> 00:00:47,859
The Normans, across the sea in northern
France, were descended from Vikings.

18
00:00:47,859 --> 00:00:52,540
Their leader, Rollo, was given
land there, traditionally in 911.

19
00:00:53,841 --> 00:00:55,685
Their very name tells the story.

20
00:00:55,685 --> 00:00:57,356
Norman simply means Northman.

21
00:00:57,356 --> 00:01:01,506
But within a few generations, these
Northmen had stopped speaking Norse.

22
00:01:01,506 --> 00:01:02,543
They spoke French.

23
00:01:03,844 --> 00:01:05,620
And there was a link to England.

24
00:01:05,620 --> 00:01:09,954
Queen Emma, the wife of King Ethelred,
was the daughter of a duke of Normandy.

25
00:01:09,954 --> 00:01:13,676
Her son Edward grew up in exile
there, for about 25 years.

26
00:01:14,977 --> 00:01:18,686
In 1042, Edward came home
and became king of England.

27
00:01:18,686 --> 00:01:21,138
He is remembered as Edward the Confessor.

28
00:01:21,138 --> 00:01:22,275
He had no children.

29
00:01:22,275 --> 00:01:25,385
And on 5 January 1066, he died.

30
00:01:26,686 --> 00:01:28,368
What came next happened fast.

31
00:01:28,368 --> 00:01:33,126
The very next day, the day of Edward's
funeral, the most powerful earl in England,

32
00:01:33,126 --> 00:01:36,433
Harold Godwinson, was crowned
king in Westminster Abbey.

33
00:01:37,734 --> 00:01:38,973
Harold had the crown.

34
00:01:38,973 --> 00:01:42,337
But across the sea, two other
men believed it was theirs.

35
00:01:42,337 --> 00:01:44,993
In Normandy, Edward's
relative, Duke William.

36
00:01:44,993 --> 00:01:49,715
And in Norway, King Harald Hardrada, one
of the most feared warriors of his age.

37
00:01:49,715 --> 00:01:50,305
Three men.

38
00:01:50,305 --> 00:01:50,896
One crown.

39
00:01:52,195 --> 00:01:55,807
Norman writers said that Edward
had promised William the crown,

40
00:01:55,807 --> 00:01:59,418
and that Harold himself had once
sworn an oath to support him.

41
00:01:59,418 --> 00:02:01,653
English writers told a different story.

42
00:02:01,653 --> 00:02:04,120
William decided to take the crown by force.

43
00:02:05,420 --> 00:02:07,097
These men wonder at the star.

44
00:02:07,097 --> 00:02:12,770
That Latin caption on the Bayeux Tapestry shows
men pointing up at a bright star with a long tail,

45
00:02:12,770 --> 00:02:14,680
seen in late April of that year.

46
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:15,953
It was Halley's Comet.

47
00:02:17,252 --> 00:02:19,253
In September, the first blow fell.

48
00:02:19,253 --> 00:02:22,373
Harald Hardrada sailed from
Norway with a huge fleet.

49
00:02:22,373 --> 00:02:25,375
The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle says 300 ships.

50
00:02:25,375 --> 00:02:29,966
With him was Tostig, Harold's own brother,
who had been driven out of England.

51
00:02:31,265 --> 00:02:34,290
On 20 September, at
Fulford, just outside York,

52
00:02:34,290 --> 00:02:38,986
the Norwegians crushed the two northern
English earls, Edwin and Morcar.

53
00:02:38,986 --> 00:02:42,444
York made peace with the invaders,
and handed over hostages.

54
00:02:43,743 --> 00:02:46,725
Harold was in the south,
watching the coast for William.

55
00:02:46,725 --> 00:02:49,335
When the news came, he
raced north with his army.

56
00:02:49,335 --> 00:02:51,997
About 300 kilometres, in roughly a week.

57
00:02:53,298 --> 00:02:55,431
His best soldiers were the housecarls,

58
00:02:55,431 --> 00:02:59,026
professional warriors who fought
on foot with long, heavy axes.

59
00:02:59,026 --> 00:03:00,991
They pushed north, mile after mile.

60
00:03:00,991 --> 00:03:03,686
And the Norwegians had
no idea they were coming.

61
00:03:04,985 --> 00:03:09,927
On 25 September, the Norwegians were
waiting at a place called Stamford Bridge,

62
00:03:09,927 --> 00:03:13,366
east of York, for the hostages
they had been promised.

63
00:03:13,366 --> 00:03:15,475
Instead, they got a terrible surprise.

64
00:03:15,475 --> 00:03:16,420
The English army.

65
00:03:17,719 --> 00:03:20,128
There is a famous story about this battle.

66
00:03:20,128 --> 00:03:23,226
A single giant Norwegian
held the narrow bridge alone,

67
00:03:23,226 --> 00:03:26,954
until an Englishman floated under
it and stabbed him from below.

68
00:03:26,954 --> 00:03:30,912
But the story was only added to the
Chronicle in the twelfth century.

69
00:03:30,912 --> 00:03:32,288
It is probably a legend.

70
00:03:33,588 --> 00:03:35,539
The real battle was long and hard.

71
00:03:35,539 --> 00:03:37,950
But in the end, the English broke through.

72
00:03:37,950 --> 00:03:39,500
Harald Hardrada was killed.

73
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:41,566
So was Tostig, Harold's own brother.

74
00:03:42,865 --> 00:03:44,882
The Norwegian army was destroyed.

75
00:03:44,882 --> 00:03:51,171
One version of the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle says
the survivors needed only 24 ships to sail home.

76
00:03:51,171 --> 00:03:54,469
Many historians see this as
the end of the Viking Age.

77
00:03:55,770 --> 00:03:57,531
But Harold had no time to rest.

78
00:03:57,531 --> 00:04:00,656
For weeks, William had been
waiting for the right wind.

79
00:04:00,656 --> 00:04:04,306
On 28 September, just three
days after Stamford Bridge,

80
00:04:04,306 --> 00:04:07,758
the Normans landed at
Pevensey, on the south coast.

81
00:04:09,057 --> 00:04:12,515
William moved his army to Hastings,
built a wooden castle there,

82
00:04:12,515 --> 00:04:15,756
and raided the countryside
around it, to draw Harold south.

83
00:04:17,055 --> 00:04:18,841
And here is our first new word.

84
00:04:18,841 --> 00:04:19,245
Castle.

85
00:04:19,245 --> 00:04:22,644
Before the Normans, castles
were almost unknown in England.

86
00:04:22,644 --> 00:04:27,485
The word castel entered English around the
time of the conquest, from Norman French.

87
00:04:28,786 --> 00:04:30,298
Harold hurried south again.

88
00:04:30,298 --> 00:04:33,715
He gathered more men in London,
then marched to meet William.

89
00:04:33,715 --> 00:04:38,363
His army had fought one hard battle and
made two long marches in less than a month.

90
00:04:39,663 --> 00:04:42,117
14 October 1066.

91
00:04:42,117 --> 00:04:42,776
A Saturday.

92
00:04:42,776 --> 00:04:48,045
About 11 kilometres from Hastings, on a
ridge at the place now simply called Battle.

93
00:04:49,346 --> 00:04:51,923
Harold placed his army
along the top of the ridge.

94
00:04:51,923 --> 00:04:54,807
The English stood shoulder
to shoulder in a shield wall.

95
00:04:54,807 --> 00:04:57,899
If William wanted the crown,
he would have to attack uphill.

96
00:04:59,199 --> 00:05:00,826
The English fought on foot.

97
00:05:00,826 --> 00:05:04,442
Their best men, the housecarls,
swung long, two-handed axes.

98
00:05:04,442 --> 00:05:07,033
The Normans brought three kinds of soldier.

99
00:05:07,033 --> 00:05:07,514
Archers.

100
00:05:07,514 --> 00:05:08,358
Foot soldiers.

101
00:05:08,358 --> 00:05:09,865
And knights on horseback.

102
00:05:11,165 --> 00:05:13,471
The battle began around nine in the morning.

103
00:05:13,471 --> 00:05:16,983
The Norman archers shot up the
slope, and then the knights charged.

104
00:05:16,983 --> 00:05:19,185
But again and again, the shield wall held.

105
00:05:20,485 --> 00:05:21,194
The wall held.

106
00:05:21,194 --> 00:05:24,942
Hour after hour, the Normans could not
break the English line on the hill.

107
00:05:26,242 --> 00:05:28,815
Then a rumour spread
through the Norman army.

108
00:05:28,815 --> 00:05:29,846
The duke was dead.

109
00:05:29,846 --> 00:05:34,765
According to a Norman writer, William pushed
back his helmet and rode along his lines,

110
00:05:34,765 --> 00:05:36,824
so that his men could see his face.

111
00:05:36,824 --> 00:05:37,569
He was alive.

112
00:05:38,869 --> 00:05:41,689
Norman writers say that
William then used a trick.

113
00:05:41,689 --> 00:05:43,606
His knights pretended to run away.

114
00:05:43,606 --> 00:05:48,343
English soldiers chased them down the hill,
and the knights turned and cut them off.

115
00:05:48,343 --> 00:05:51,389
Historians still argue about
how much of this is true.

116
00:05:52,689 --> 00:05:54,730
Slowly, the shield wall grew thinner.

117
00:05:54,730 --> 00:05:59,088
Late in the day, as the light began to
fade, the Normans broke through at last.

118
00:06:00,389 --> 00:06:03,321
Somewhere in that last fight,
King Harold was killed.

119
00:06:03,321 --> 00:06:06,585
The Bayeux Tapestry shows a
man with an arrow near his eye,

120
00:06:06,585 --> 00:06:09,074
under the words: here King Harold is killed.

121
00:06:09,074 --> 00:06:13,997
But no one is sure that this man is Harold,
and later repairs may have changed the arrow.

122
00:06:15,298 --> 00:06:16,790
By nightfall, it was over.

123
00:06:16,790 --> 00:06:20,292
Harold was dead, and so were
his brothers Gyrth and Leofwine.

124
00:06:20,292 --> 00:06:23,851
In one day, England had lost its
king and many of its leaders.

125
00:06:25,151 --> 00:06:28,579
A few years later, William built
an abbey on the battlefield.

126
00:06:28,579 --> 00:06:31,894
Tradition says its high altar
was placed where Harold fell.

127
00:06:31,894 --> 00:06:34,985
The town that grew up around
it is still called Battle.

128
00:06:36,284 --> 00:06:40,367
In London, the English leaders chose
a young prince, Edgar, as their king.

129
00:06:40,367 --> 00:06:41,747
But he was never crowned.

130
00:06:41,747 --> 00:06:46,437
As William's army closed in, they came out
to meet him at Berkhamsted, and submitted.

131
00:06:47,737 --> 00:06:49,173
Crowned on Christmas Day.

132
00:06:49,173 --> 00:06:55,204
On 25 December 1066, William was crowned
king of England in Westminster Abbey.

133
00:06:56,504 --> 00:06:58,478
Something strange happened inside.

134
00:06:58,478 --> 00:07:02,428
The crowd shouted its approval in
two languages, English and French.

135
00:07:02,428 --> 00:07:04,926
Outside, the Norman guards heard the noise,

136
00:07:04,926 --> 00:07:08,470
thought it was an attack, and
set fire to the houses nearby.

137
00:07:09,769 --> 00:07:10,954
Look at that year.

138
00:07:10,954 --> 00:07:12,466
Edward dies in January.

139
00:07:12,466 --> 00:07:13,913
Fulford, in September.

140
00:07:13,913 --> 00:07:16,081
Stamford Bridge, five days later.

141
00:07:16,081 --> 00:07:17,463
Hastings, in October.

142
00:07:17,463 --> 00:07:19,303
And a new king at Christmas.

143
00:07:19,303 --> 00:07:21,737
Three crowned kings in a single year.

144
00:07:23,036 --> 00:07:26,146
William gave the land of
England to his Norman followers.

145
00:07:26,146 --> 00:07:29,529
By the time of the great survey
called the Domesday Book,

146
00:07:29,529 --> 00:07:32,913
in 1086, very few of the great
landholders were English.

147
00:07:34,213 --> 00:07:36,295
So now England had three languages.

148
00:07:36,295 --> 00:07:38,497
The king and his nobles spoke French.

149
00:07:38,497 --> 00:07:41,473
The main written language
of government was Latin.

150
00:07:41,473 --> 00:07:46,413
And most ordinary people, the farmers,
servants and craftsmen, still spoke English.

151
00:07:47,713 --> 00:07:48,689
English survived.

152
00:07:48,689 --> 00:07:53,798
At Peterborough Abbey, monks kept writing
their chronicle in English until 1154,

153
00:07:53,798 --> 00:07:56,096
almost ninety years after Hastings.

154
00:07:57,396 --> 00:08:02,057
And in that chronicle, in the early
1100s, we find a new word: werre.

155
00:08:02,057 --> 00:08:05,362
It came from Norman French,
and today we spell it W-A-R.

156
00:08:05,362 --> 00:08:05,598
Yes.

157
00:08:05,598 --> 00:08:08,606
Even the word war came to
English with the Normans.

158
00:08:08,606 --> 00:08:12,264
And the French had first borrowed
it from a Germanic language.

159
00:08:13,564 --> 00:08:14,360
Cow and beef.

160
00:08:14,360 --> 00:08:15,276
Swine and pork.

161
00:08:15,276 --> 00:08:16,317
Sheep and mutton.

162
00:08:16,317 --> 00:08:17,173
Calf and veal.

163
00:08:17,173 --> 00:08:19,985
These are the most famous
Norman words of all.

164
00:08:19,985 --> 00:08:23,288
The animals in the field
kept their old English names,

165
00:08:23,288 --> 00:08:25,980
but the meat on the table got French names.

166
00:08:27,279 --> 00:08:30,336
The usual story: English
farmers raised the animals,

167
00:08:30,336 --> 00:08:32,687
and French-speaking lords ate the meat.

168
00:08:32,687 --> 00:08:37,448
The novelist Walter Scott made this
idea famous in Ivanhoe, in 1819.

169
00:08:37,448 --> 00:08:38,683
The truth is messier.

170
00:08:38,683 --> 00:08:41,774
These words arrived
slowly, mostly around 1300,

171
00:08:41,774 --> 00:08:45,326
and at first, beef could
also mean the living animal.

172
00:08:46,625 --> 00:08:49,405
The same thing happened wherever power was.

173
00:08:49,405 --> 00:08:50,116
Government.

174
00:08:50,116 --> 00:08:50,504
Court.

175
00:08:50,504 --> 00:08:50,892
Judge.

176
00:08:50,892 --> 00:08:51,216
Jury.

177
00:08:51,216 --> 00:08:51,732
Justice.

178
00:08:51,732 --> 00:08:52,120
Crime.

179
00:08:52,120 --> 00:08:52,572
Prison.

180
00:08:52,572 --> 00:08:52,895
Army.

181
00:08:52,895 --> 00:08:53,219
Navy.

182
00:08:53,219 --> 00:08:53,735
Soldier.

183
00:08:53,735 --> 00:08:54,124
Enemy.

184
00:08:54,124 --> 00:08:54,511
Peace.

185
00:08:54,511 --> 00:08:57,292
All of these came into English from French.

186
00:08:58,591 --> 00:09:03,368
In all, some 10,000 French words came into
English in the centuries after the conquest.

187
00:09:03,368 --> 00:09:06,142
And about three quarters of
them are still used today.

188
00:09:07,442 --> 00:09:10,734
This is why English often
has two words for one idea.

189
00:09:10,734 --> 00:09:13,716
The short, everyday word
is usually Old English.

190
00:09:13,716 --> 00:09:16,448
The longer, more formal one is often French.

191
00:09:16,448 --> 00:09:17,692
Begin, and commence.

192
00:09:17,692 --> 00:09:18,684
Ask, and demand.

193
00:09:18,684 --> 00:09:19,990
Freedom, and liberty.

194
00:09:19,990 --> 00:09:20,860
Help, and aid.

195
00:09:22,159 --> 00:09:25,335
The Normans even changed
how English was written.

196
00:09:25,335 --> 00:09:29,094
French-trained scribes spelled
the old English cw as qu.

197
00:09:29,094 --> 00:09:30,455
So cwen became queen.

198
00:09:30,455 --> 00:09:34,019
And they used ch for the
sound at the start of church.

199
00:09:35,320 --> 00:09:37,147
And the result is still with us.

200
00:09:37,147 --> 00:09:41,680
In one famous study of about 80,000
words in an English dictionary,

201
00:09:41,680 --> 00:09:44,115
around 28 percent came from French.

202
00:09:44,115 --> 00:09:47,201
Only about a quarter came
from the old Germanic roots.

203
00:09:48,500 --> 00:09:49,960
So the Normans won the war.

204
00:09:49,960 --> 00:09:52,392
But they never defeated
the English language.

205
00:09:52,392 --> 00:09:53,959
The farmers kept speaking it.

206
00:09:53,959 --> 00:09:58,120
And slowly, the words of their conquerors
flowed into it, and made it richer.

207
00:09:59,421 --> 00:10:00,312
The longest war.

208
00:10:00,312 --> 00:10:04,097
That is what it would take for
English to win back the king's court:

209
00:10:04,097 --> 00:10:08,105
a war between England and France that
lasted more than a hundred years.

210
00:10:08,105 --> 00:10:09,830
That is the story of part four.

